course: dictionary
lesson: 9403
title: Words from space
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can tell its own story. Sputnik came into English from Russian, and it now names a machine that travels round the Earth. The words around it give you clues: launched, orbit and beeped all point to space.
explain: «In 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, the first artificial satellite, a metal ball that beeped as it circled the Earth.» Artificial means made by people, not by nature. Ask yourself what word would fit, then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some words carry a feeling as well as a meaning. A Sputnik moment is a sudden shock that makes people work harder. The shock is the clue: the phrase is not about a ball in the sky, but about a surprise that changes what people do.
fact: Sputnik is a Russian word that means «fellow traveller» or «satellite».
fact: A satellite is a machine that travels round a bigger object in space, such as the Earth.
fact: «Orbit» means the path a satellite follows round the Earth.
fact: Sputnik 1 was the first man-made satellite sent into orbit, launched by the Soviet Union in 1957.
fact: A «Sputnik moment» is a sudden shock that makes people work harder.
exercise: «In 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, the first artificial satellite.» What does artificial mean here? | *Made by people, not by nature | Very, very old | Found deep in a cave | Made of wood
exercise: «The first satellite, Sputnik 1, was launched in 1957.» What does launched mean here? | Painted bright red | Lost in a storm | Cooked slowly in an oven | *Sent up into space
exercise: Ben says a Sputnik moment is a quiet night with stars. What does it really mean? | A beep from a radio | A quiet night with stars | *A sudden shock that makes people work harder | A party with fireworks
test: «The Soviet satellite Sputnik shocked the United States.» What does shocked mean here? | Made to laugh | Hit by electricity | *Surprised and upset | Very hungry
test: What is a satellite? | *A machine that travels round a bigger object in space | A small country far away | A Russian dance | A kind of rocket fuel
exam: «Sputnik 1 was sent into orbit around the Earth.» What does orbit mean here? | A loud beep from a radio | A kind of rocket | A country far away | *The path a satellite follows round the Earth
